Localized contact skin irritation from prolonged use of strong adhesive bandages
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Geometric sharply demarcated erythema, Superficial epidermal peeling, Pruritus isolated to bandage area

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-010
Reason for Visit: Acute localized skin reaction requiring assessment to distinguish between contact dermatitis and evolving wound infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sharply demarcated erythema and skin peeling around a previous minor wound site. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to confidently rule out spreading cellulitis and to manage the acute pruritic distress to prevent secondary mechanical excoriation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin where I had the band-aid on is bright red, peeling, and incredibly itchy.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sharply demarcated geometric patch of erythema mirroring the shape of an adhesive bandage applied 3 days ago. Describes superficial epidermal peeling upon removal of the adhesive. Reports mild localized pruritus isolated strictly to the contact area. Notes transient stinging upon application of water or soap. Intact skin barrier over the original minor wound without ulceration. Absence of systemic allergic symptoms, shortness of breath, or facial swelling.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is moderate, causing distraction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, localized discomfort.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals a sharply demarcated rectangular patch of erythema matching a standard bandage size. Diagnostic triad confirmed: geometric sharply demarcated erythema, superficial epidermal peeling, and pruritus isolated to the bandage area. Residual sticky adhesive residue is visible on the skin surface. Normal surrounding unaffected skin. Lack of spreading urticaria beyond the contact zone. Original minor wound is clean and healing well.
Environmental Observations: Box of strong waterproof adhesive bandages on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No new systemic medications or known allergies documented.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Localized contact skin irritation from prolonged use of strong adhesive bandages - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Erythema is strictly geometric and confined to the adhesive area, without deep warmth or induration.

Systemic Allergic Reaction (Anaphylaxis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of airway, breathing, or systemic circulatory compromise.

Herpes Simplex/Zoster - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of clustered vesicles and direct correlation to adhesive application.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient developed acute, intensely pruritic and erythematous skin breakdown mimicking early cellulitis. Prompt face-to-face assessment was required to definitively diagnose irritant/allergic contact dermatitis versus an infectious process. By confirming the geometric nature of the lesion and the absence of systemic or deep tissue involvement, the assessment successfully avoided inappropriate antibiotic use and safely managed the localized reaction with topical therapies, fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Gentle removal of residual adhesive using a medical adhesive remover or mineral oil. Application of a mild topical corticosteroid (e.g., 1% hydrocortisone cream) to reduce localized inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Transition to using paper tape or silicone-based adhesives for future wound care to prevent recurrence of irritant dermatitis, reinforcing this by discarding the offending waterproof bandages immediately.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Silicone-based low-allergy dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the erythema spreads beyond the original bandage lines.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop swelling of the lips or tongue, difficulty breathing, or a widespread hive-like rash across the body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a localized contact reaction; identifying the specific chemical irritant requires formal patch testing if reactions are recurrent.
Clinical Course: The redness and itching should significantly improve within 48 to 72 hours with topical steroid application and avoidance of the adhesive.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the red area begins to expand rapidly, becomes hot, or if pus develops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chemicals in the bandage adhesive caused an inflammatory reaction in the top layer of skin. The steroid cream calms the immune cells causing the redness and itch.
Actionable Steps: Do not scrub the area with soap. Apply the steroid cream twice daily. Leave the area open to the air to heal.

